{#
  NTP client -- IP Infusion OcNOS.

  WHY THIS DEVICE HAS ONE: ocnos runs under vrnetlab, so it is a QEMU VM with its own clock
  rather than a container sharing the host's. Measured on a live o1: before this template was
  applied `show ntp peer-status` reported "No association IDs returned" (no time source at
  all), and the first poll after it was applied measured an offset of 1295.649 ms. Smaller
  than the n9kv's 4m08s, but that node had been up far longer -- drift ACCUMULATES with
  uptime, so a freshly booted VM being close to the host proves nothing about a long-lived one.

  COMMANDS: grounded in the OcNOS 7.0 System Management Guide, "NTP Client" configuration.

  WHY THERE IS NO `ntp enable vrf management` HERE, though the guide lists it: OcNOS ships
  with NTP already enabled, and re-issuing the command on a device where it is already on
  answers "%% Given configuration already exists", which the deploy task treats as a failure.
  That fails `netlab up` as a COMMAND -- it exits non-zero and stops before the lab is usable.
  It does NOT abort the Ansible play: there is no `any_errors_fatal` anywhere in
  netsim/ansible, so every other host finishes its own configuration first. The lab is left
  half-configured rather than untouched, which is the more annoying of the two outcomes.

  Verified on a live node: `feature ntp` and `ntp server` are both idempotent (re-applying
  either is clean), `ntp enable` is not. If you ever meet an OcNOS build that ships with NTP
  disabled, this template will need it back -- guarded, not unconditional.

  VRF: OcNOS runs NTP on the management VRF by default, and netlab's OcNOS management
  interface is eth0 in `vrf management` -- the same VRF the initial template already uses for
  `feature streaming-telemetry vrf management`.

  NO `commit` HERE: the OcNOS deployment task (ansible/tasks/deploy-config/ocnos.yml) issues
  the commit itself and carries the logic that distinguishes an advisory response from a real
  failure. A commit here would duplicate it and defeat that handling.

  VERIFY WITH `show ntp peer-status`: a leading `*` on the server line means selected for
  sync. The presence of the config is not evidence that it worked.
#}
{#
  ---- SHARED NOTE (kept in both ntp templates) --------------------------------------------
  THE SERVER ADDRESS IS DERIVED, NOT HARD-CODED. It is the lab's management gateway, which on
  any netlab host is the host itself -- the box where chronyd runs. Deriving it keeps this
  correct under any multilab id (the mgmt network is 192.168.<id>.0/24) and on any netlab
  host, instead of pinning one box's address into the fork.

  THE TWO RENDER CONTEXTS ARE NOT THE SAME, and this is the trap that cost the first attempt:
    * netlab renders this at `netlab create` time with its OWN Jinja environment, where the
      management pool is `addressing.mgmt.ipv4`; `pools` does NOT exist.
    * Ansible renders it again at `netlab initial` time, where the same data is `pools.mgmt`
      (outputs/ansible.py copies topology.addressing into `pools`); `addressing` does NOT
      exist.
  A template using only one of them fails in the other context. Using only `pools` failed at
  create time with a bare "'width' is undefined" -- netaddr choking on an Undefined value,
  an error that names nothing useful. Hence the explicit either/or below.
#}
{% set _mgmt_pool = addressing.mgmt.ipv4 if addressing is defined else pools.mgmt.ipv4 %}
feature ntp vrf management
!
ntp server {{ _mgmt_pool | ipaddr('1') | ipaddr('address') }} vrf management

